        "titulo": "Four letter traps — and two new mouth positions",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: German W usually sounds /v/, as in English very: Wasser. STEP 2: V often sounds /f/, as in father: Vater; loanwords such as Vase may use /v/. STEP 3: J sounds /j/, the first sound in English yes: Jahr. STEP 4: Z sounds /ts/, like the end of English cats: Zeit. STEP 5: German S often sounds /z/ before a vowel at the beginning of a syllable, as in Sonne; in other positions it can sound /s/. STEP 6: To make Ü, position your tongue as for English ee and round the lips; Ö combines a front e-like tongue position with rounded lips. Neither is simply an English oo. Listen rather than trusting approximations.",
